WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.393
- What is quantum mechanics?

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.850
- Quantum mechanics is
the paradigm of physics

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.290
that came into being in the
early part of the 20th century

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.100
that replaced classical mechanics.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.200
And it replaced classical
mechanics in a weird way

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.850
that we're still coming to terms with.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.367
So in classic mechanics,
you have an object,

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.542
it has a location, it has a velocity,

00:00:22.542 --> 00:00:24.360
and if you know the location and velocity

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.240
of everything in the world,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.640
you can say what everything's going to do.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.880
Quantum mechanics has an aspect of it

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.060
that is kind of on the same line,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.680
so there's something
called the quantum state,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.840
or the wave function,
and there's an equation

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.620
governing what the quantum state does.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.470
So it's very much like
classical mechanics.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.100
The wave function is different,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.290
it's sort of a wave, it's a vector,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.450
in a huge dimensional vector space,

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.820
rather than a position or velocity,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.460
but okay, that's a detail.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.160
And the equation is, the
Schrodinger Equation,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.110
not Newton's law, is, but
okay, again, a detail.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.870
Where quantum mechanics really
becomes weird and different

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.420
is that there's a whole
nother set of rules

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.780
in our textbook formulation
of quantum mechanics,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.320
in addition to saying that
there's a quantum state

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.590
and it evolves in time.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.450
And all these new rules have to do with

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.280
what happens when you look at this system,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.540
when you observe it, when you measure it.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.920
In classical mechanics, there
were no rules about observing.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.560
You just look at it, and
you see what's going on,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.010
that was it, right?

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.240
In quantum mechanics, the way we teach it,

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.260
there's something profoundly fundamental

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.630
about the act of
measurement or observation,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.610
and the system dramatically
changes its state.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.350
Even though it has a wave function,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.400
like the electron and an atom
is not orbiting in a circle,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.880
it's sort of spread out in a cloud.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.880
When you look at it, you
don't see that cloud.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:39.920
When you look at it,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.270
it looks like a particle with a location.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.100
So it dramatically changes
its state right away.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.660
And the affects of that
change can be instantly seen

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.187
in what the electron does next.

00:01:49.187 --> 00:01:53.580
So that's the, again,
we need to be careful

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.470
because we don't agree on
what quantum mechanics says,

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.380
that's why I need to say,
like, in the textbook views,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.603
et cetera, right, but
in the textbook view,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:05.010
quantum mechanics, unlike
any other theory of physics,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.993
places, gives a fundamental
role to the act of measurement.

